--  The network: internet addresses and their text, and a concept beside
--  core/io's and outside `world` through which a program connects,
--  listens, accepts and sends and receives over TCP and UDP.  A provider
--  without a network, and a firmware image that never asks for one,
--  carries none of it.  Resolving a name to an address is not here.

import core/io
import core/mem
import core/text
import core/time

--- The text of an address could not be read as one: not four decimal
--- parts from 0 to 255, not an IPv6 address's groups, a zone, or a port
--- that is missing, above 65535 or not decimal.
public invalid_address: atom
--- Nothing listens at the address a connection was asked for: the system
--- answered it at once, as a closed port is answered.
public refused: atom
--- No route leads to the address: its network or its host cannot be
--- reached from here.
public unreachable: atom
--- Another socket already holds the address and port: a listener or a
--- datagram socket bound there.
public address_in_use: atom
--- The address cannot be used here: no interface of this system holds it,
--- or the system does not have its family at all, as one without IPv6
--- does not.
public address_unavailable: atom
--- The time limit `set_timeouts` gave passed before anything arrived or
--- could be sent, or a connection's handshake took longer than the system
--- waits.  The socket is still usable.
public timed_out: atom
--- The connection's peer has gone: it closed or reset the connection, so
--- what is sent cannot be delivered.  A write to such a connection is this
--- atom on a hosted system, never the end of the process.
public disconnected: atom

--- An IPv4 address: four bytes, `127.0.0.1` in its text.
public ipv4: atom
--- An IPv6 address: sixteen bytes, `::1` in its text.
public ipv6: atom
--- What an address is: `ipv4` or `ipv6`.
public address_family: type = ipv4 | ipv6

--- An internet address and a port: TCP's and UDP's endpoint.  `family`
--- says which; an IPv4 address is `octets`' first four bytes, in the
--- order its text writes them, and the other twelve are zero; an IPv6
--- address is all sixteen, in network order.  `port` is a number from 0
--- to 65535, and 0 asks the system for a free one when a socket is bound.
--- There is no zone: an IPv6 link-local address that needs one, `fe80::1%eth0`,
--- cannot be written.  Make one with `ipv4_address`, `ipv6_address`,
--- `loopback`, `unspecified` or `parse_address`, and compare two with
--- `same_address`.
public address: type = struct
    family: address_family
    octets: [16]u8
    port: u16
end address

--- What arrived in one `receive_from`: how many bytes, and the address and
--- port of the socket that sent them.
public datagram: type = struct
    count: usize
    sender: address
end datagram

--- Stop receiving: what arrives later is thrown away.
public receiving: atom
--- Stop sending: the peer reads the end of the stream once what was sent
--- before has arrived, while this side may still receive.
public sending: atom
--- Stop both receiving and sending.
public both_directions: atom
--- Which way of a connection `shutdown` stops: `receiving`, `sending` or
--- `both_directions`.
public direction: type = receiving | sending | both_directions

--- The network: TCP connections and UDP datagram sockets over IPv4 and
--- IPv6, each a `file` of the provider.  It is a reader and a writer of
--- them as well, so a connection is read and written through `io.read`
--- and `io.write`, a line reader and a buffered writer among them, whose
--- failures are `io_failed` with the provider's detail; `send` and
--- `receive` say more, `timed_out` and `disconnected`.  A connection's
--- `read` and `receive` return zero once its peer has finished sending.
---
--- Every call waits until it can do what it was asked, as a blocking
--- socket does: `accept` for a connection, `receive` for bytes, `send` for
--- room, unless `set_timeouts` gave the socket a limit.  A listener takes
--- connections whose handshake the system completes on its own, so one
--- thread can connect to its own listener and then accept, and a
--- connection's sends wait only once the system's buffers are full.
--- Close every socket with `close_socket`; one dropped unclosed is open
--- until the process ends, since there are no destructors.
public network: type = concept (provider: type) is io.reader, io.writer
    --- Connect to a listener at `to` and return the connection.  A port
    --- nothing listens on is `refused`.
    connect: (self: provider, to: address) -> (connected: io.file)
             ! refused | unreachable | address_unavailable | timed_out
             | io.io_failed
    --- Listen for connections at `at`, port 0 for a free one, which
    --- `local_address` then gives.  A listener may take an address its
    --- earlier connections still hold while they close, never one another
    --- socket listens at, `address_in_use`.
    listen: (self: provider, at: address) -> (listener: io.file)
            ! address_in_use | address_unavailable | io.no_access | io.io_failed
    --- Take the next connection made to a listener, waiting for one.
    accept: (self: provider, listener: io.file) -> (accepted: io.file)
            ! timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Open a UDP socket bound to `at`, port 0 for a free one.
    open_datagram: (self: provider, at: address) -> (socket: io.file)
                   ! address_in_use | address_unavailable | io.no_access
                   | io.io_failed
    --- Send all of `bytes` over a connection, waiting while its buffers
    --- are full.  A failure may follow a part of them.
    send: (self: provider, connection: io.file, bytes: []u8) -> none
          ! disconnected | timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Receive what arrives next on a connection into `into`, waiting for
    --- something: its count, zero once the peer has finished sending.
    receive: (self: provider, connection: io.file, into: []mut u8)
             -> (count: usize) ! disconnected | timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Send `bytes` as one datagram to `to`.  One that cannot be sent
    --- whole, longer than the system takes, is `io_failed`.
    send_to: (self: provider, socket: io.file, bytes: []u8, to: address)
             -> none ! unreachable | timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Receive the next datagram into `into`, waiting for one, and say
    --- who sent it.  A datagram longer than `into` is cut to it, the rest
    --- thrown away.
    receive_from: (self: provider, socket: io.file, into: []mut u8)
                  -> (received: datagram) ! timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Stop receiving, sending or both on a connection.
    shutdown: (self: provider, connection: io.file, stopping: direction)
              -> none ! io.io_failed
    --- The address and port a socket is bound to.
    local_address: (self: provider, socket: io.file) -> (at: address)
                   ! io.io_failed
    --- The address and port of a connection's peer.
    peer_address: (self: provider, connection: io.file) -> (at: address)
                  ! io.io_failed
    --- Limit how long a receive or an accept, and a send, waits on
    --- `socket`; a span of zero is no limit.
    set_timeouts: (self: provider, socket: io.file, receive_limit: time.duration,
                   send_limit: time.duration) -> none ! io.io_failed
    --- Send a connection's small writes at once, `on`, rather than gather
    --- them while an earlier one is unacknowledged.
    set_no_delay: (self: provider, connection: io.file, on: bool) -> none
                  ! io.io_failed
    --- Close a socket, which is consumed even when this fails.
    close_socket: (self: provider, sink socket: io.file) -> none ! io.io_failed
end network

--- An IPv4 address `a.b.c.d` and a port.
public ipv4_address: (a: u8, b: u8, c: u8, d: u8, port: u16)
                     -> (at: address) =
    at = (family: ipv4, octets: zeroed, port: port)
    at.octets[0] = a
    at.octets[1] = b
    at.octets[2] = c
    at.octets[3] = d
end ipv4_address

--- An IPv6 address of eight 16-bit groups, in the order its text writes
--- them, and a port.
public ipv6_address: (groups: [8]u16, port: u16) -> (at: address) =
    at = (family: ipv6, octets: zeroed, port: port)
    for index in usize(0)..<8 do
        at.octets[2 * index] = u8(groups[index] >> 8)
        at.octets[2 * index + 1] = u8(groups[index] & 255)
    end for
end ipv6_address

--- The loopback address of `family`, `127.0.0.1` or `::1`, and a port:
--- this system, reached without a network.
public loopback: (family: address_family, port: u16) -> (at: address) =
    at = (family: family, octets: zeroed, port: port)
    if family == ipv4 then
        at.octets[0] = 127
        at.octets[3] = 1
    else
        at.octets[15] = 1
    end if
end loopback

--- The unspecified address of `family`, `0.0.0.0` or `::`, and a port: a
--- socket bound to it takes what arrives at the port on every interface.
public unspecified: (family: address_family, port: u16) -> (at: address) =
    at = (family: family, octets: zeroed, port: port)
end unspecified

--- The address `at` with another port.
public with_port: (at: address, port: u16) -> (result: address) =
    result = at
    result.port = port
end with_port

--- Whether two addresses are the same family, address and port.
public same_address: (left: address, right: address) -> (same: bool) =
    same = left.family == right.family and left.port == right.port
    for index in usize(0)..<16 do
        if left.octets[index] <> right.octets[index] then
            same = false
        end if
    end for
end same_address

--- Whether an address is its family's unspecified address, whatever its
--- port.
public is_unspecified: (at: address) -> (yes: bool) =
    yes = true
    for index in usize(0)..<16 do
        if at.octets[index] <> 0 then yes = false end if
    end for
end is_unspecified

--  An IPv4 address's four parts: decimal, from 0 to 255, with no leading
--  zero, which some readers take for octal.  Each part is core/text's to
--  parse.
ipv4_octets: (source: []u8) -> (octets: [4]u8) ! invalid_address =
    octets = zeroed
    mut part: usize = 0
    mut start: usize = 0
    for at in usize(0)..<lenof source + 1 do
        if at == lenof source or source[at] == u8('.') then
            fail invalid_address when part == 4
            piece: []u8 = source[start..<at]
            fail invalid_address when lenof piece == 0
              or (lenof piece > 1 and piece[0] == u8('0'))
            octets[part] = text.parse_u8(piece) else (problem)
                _ = problem
                fail invalid_address
            end
            inc part
            start = at + 1
        end if
    end for
    fail invalid_address when part <> 4
end ipv4_octets

--  Where in SOURCE, from AT, the next `:` is, or its length.
next_colon: (source: []u8, at: usize) -> (found: usize) =
    found = at
    while found < lenof source and source[found] <> u8(':') do
        inc found
    end while
end next_colon

--  An IPv6 address's sixteen bytes, from its groups: one to four
--  hexadecimal digits each, at most one `::` standing for one or more
--  zero groups, and an IPv4 address in the last 32 bits.
ipv6_octets: (source: []u8) -> (octets: [16]u8) ! invalid_address =
    octets = zeroed
    mut groups: [8]u16 = zeroed
    mut count: usize = 0
    mut gap: usize = 9
    mut at: usize = 0
    length: usize = lenof source
    fail invalid_address when length < 2
    if source[0] == u8(':') then
        fail invalid_address when source[1] <> u8(':')
        gap = 0
        at = 2
    end if
    while at < length do
        fail invalid_address when count == 8
        ends: usize = next_colon(source, at)
        piece: []u8 = source[at..<ends]
        if ends == length and lenof piece > 4 then
            --  An IPv4 address is the last 32 bits, and nothing follows it.
            fail invalid_address when count > 6
            four: [4]u8 = try ipv4_octets(piece)
            groups[count] = (u16(four[0]) << 8) | u16(four[1])
            groups[count + 1] = (u16(four[2]) << 8) | u16(four[3])
            count += 2
            at = length
            break
        end if
        fail invalid_address when lenof piece == 0 or lenof piece > 4
        value: u32 = text.parse_hex_u32(piece) else (problem)
            _ = problem
            fail invalid_address
        end
        groups[count] = u16(value)
        inc count
        at = ends
        break when at == length
        --  A `:` follows the group: another group, or a second `:`.
        inc at
        fail invalid_address when at == length
        if source[at] == u8(':') then
            fail invalid_address when gap <> 9
            gap = count
            inc at
        end if
    end while
    if gap == 9 then
        fail invalid_address when count <> 8
    else
        fail invalid_address when count > 7
        --  The groups after the gap move to the end.
        moved: usize = count - gap
        mut index: usize = 0
        while index < moved do
            groups[7 - index] = groups[count - 1 - index]
            groups[count - 1 - index] = 0
            inc index
        end while
    end if
    for index in usize(0)..<8 do
        octets[2 * index] = u8(groups[index] >> 8)
        octets[2 * index + 1] = u8(groups[index] & 255)
    end for
end ipv6_octets

--- Read an address without a port from its text, `127.0.0.1` or
--- `2001:db8::1`, with port 0: four decimal parts from 0 to 255 without a
--- leading zero, or an IPv6 address's groups of one to four hexadecimal
--- digits in either case, at most one `::` and an IPv4 address in the last
--- 32 bits.  Anything else, a zone such as `%eth0` and surrounding spaces
--- among it, is `invalid_address`.
public parse_ip: (source: []u8) -> (at: address) ! invalid_address =
    at = unspecified(ipv4, 0)
    if mem.find(source, u8(':')) < lenof source then
        at.family = ipv6
        at.octets = try ipv6_octets(source)
        return
    end if
    four: [4]u8 = try ipv4_octets(source)
    for index in usize(0)..<4 do
        at.octets[index] = four[index]
    end for
end parse_ip

--- Read an address and a port from their text: `127.0.0.1:8080`, or an
--- IPv6 address in brackets, `[::1]:8080`, as a URL writes them.  The
--- port is decimal, from 0 to 65535; an address is read as `parse_ip`
--- reads one.  Anything else is `invalid_address`.
public parse_address: (source: []u8) -> (at: address) ! invalid_address =
    length: usize = lenof source
    mut colon: usize = length
    mut index: usize = length
    while index > 0 do
        dec index
        if source[index] == u8(':') then
            colon = index
            break
        end if
    end while
    fail invalid_address when colon == length or colon + 1 == length
    port: u16 = text.parse_u16(source[colon + 1..<length]) else (problem)
        _ = problem
        fail invalid_address
    end
    host: []u8 = source[0..<colon]
    if lenof host > 0 and host[0] == u8('[') then
        fail invalid_address when lenof host < 2
          or host[lenof host - 1] <> u8(']')
        at = (family: ipv6, octets: try ipv6_octets(host[1..<lenof host - 1]),
              port: port)
        return
    end if
    four: [4]u8 = try ipv4_octets(host)
    at = ipv4_address(four[0], four[1], four[2], four[3], port)
end parse_address

--  The IPv6 address's group INDEX.
group: (at: address, index: usize) -> (value: u32) =
    value = (u32(at.octets[2 * index]) << 8) | u32(at.octets[2 * index + 1])
end group

--  Append the IPv4 address in the four bytes from FIRST.
dotted: (inout into: text.builder, at: address, first: usize)
        -> none ! text.no_space =
    for index in usize(0)..<4 do
        if index > 0 then try text.append_byte(into, u8('.')) end if
        try text.append_u32(into, u32(at.octets[first + index]))
    end for
end dotted

--  Append an IPv6 address as RFC 5952 writes it: lower-case groups
--  without leading zeros, the longest run of two or more zero groups, the
--  first of equals, written `::`, and an IPv4-mapped address's last 32
--  bits in dotted decimal.
grouped: (inout into: text.builder, at: address) -> none ! text.no_space =
    mut mapped: bool = group(at, 5) == 65535
    for index in usize(0)..<5 do
        if group(at, index) <> 0 then mapped = false end if
    end for
    last: usize = if mapped then 6 else 8 end if
    mut best_start: usize = 8
    mut best_length: usize = 1
    mut index: usize = 0
    while index < last do
        if group(at, index) == 0 then
            mut ends: usize = index
            while ends < last and group(at, ends) == 0 do
                inc ends
            end while
            if ends - index > best_length then
                best_start = index
                best_length = ends - index
            end if
            index = ends
        else
            inc index
        end if
    end while
    index = 0
    while index < last do
        if index == best_start then
            try text.append(into, "::")
            index += best_length
        else
            if index > 0 and index <> best_start + best_length then
                try text.append_byte(into, u8(':'))
            end if
            try text.append_hex_u32(into, group(at, index))
            inc index
        end if
    end while
    if mapped then
        if best_start + best_length <> 6 then
            try text.append_byte(into, u8(':'))
        end if
        try dotted(into, at, 12)
    end if
end grouped

--- Append an address's text without its port: `127.0.0.1`, or an IPv6
--- address as RFC 5952 writes it, `::1` and `2001:db8::1`, lower case with
--- the longest run of zero groups shortened and an IPv4-mapped address as
--- `::ffff:192.0.2.1`.  `parse_ip` reads it back.  It is written whole or
--- not at all.
public append_ip: (inout into: text.builder, at: address)
                  -> none ! text.no_space =
    start: usize = text.length(into)
    address_written(into, at, false) else (problem)
        text.truncate(into, start)
        fail problem
    end
end append_ip

--- Append an address's text with its port: `127.0.0.1:8080`, or an IPv6
--- address in brackets, `[::1]:8080`.  `parse_address` reads it back.  It
--- is written whole or not at all.
public append_address: (inout into: text.builder, at: address)
                       -> none ! text.no_space =
    start: usize = text.length(into)
    address_written(into, at, true) else (problem)
        text.truncate(into, start)
        fail problem
    end
end append_address

address_written: (inout into: text.builder, at: address, with_port: bool)
                 -> none ! text.no_space =
    bracketed: bool = with_port and at.family == ipv6
    if bracketed then try text.append_byte(into, u8('[')) end if
    if at.family == ipv4 then
        try dotted(into, at, 0)
    else
        try grouped(into, at)
    end if
    if bracketed then try text.append_byte(into, u8(']')) end if
    if with_port then
        try text.append_byte(into, u8(':'))
        try text.append_u32(into, u32(at.port))
    end if
end address_written

address_text: (self: ptr address, inout into: text.builder)
              -> none ! text.no_space =
    try append_address(into, self.val)
end address_text

address is text.formattable (append: address_text)

--- Connect to a listener at `to` through `network` evidence.  Close the
--- connection with `close_socket`.
public connect: (provider: type is network, host: provider, to: address)
                -> (connected: io.file)
                ! refused | unreachable | address_unavailable | timed_out
                | io.io_failed =
    connected = try provider.connect(host, to)
end connect

--- Listen for connections at `at`, port 0 for a free one; close the
--- listener with `close_socket`.
public listen: (provider: type is network, host: provider, at: address)
               -> (listener: io.file)
               ! address_in_use | address_unavailable | io.no_access
               | io.io_failed =
    listener = try provider.listen(host, at)
end listen

--- Take the next connection made to `listener`, waiting for one unless a
--- receive limit was set.
public accept: (provider: type is network, host: provider, listener: io.file)
               -> (accepted: io.file) ! timed_out | io.io_failed =
    accepted = try provider.accept(host, listener)
end accept

--- Open a UDP socket bound to `at`, port 0 for a free one.
public open_datagram: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                       at: address) -> (socket: io.file)
                      ! address_in_use | address_unavailable | io.no_access
                      | io.io_failed =
    socket = try provider.open_datagram(host, at)
end open_datagram

--- Send all of `bytes` over a connection.  A connection whose peer has
--- gone is `disconnected`.
public send: (provider: type is network, host: provider, connection: io.file,
              bytes: []u8) -> none ! disconnected | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    try provider.send(host, connection, bytes)
end send

--- Receive what arrives next on a connection into `into`: its count, zero
--- once the peer has finished sending.
public receive: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                 connection: io.file, into: []mut u8)
                -> (count: usize) ! disconnected | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    count = try provider.receive(host, connection, into)
end receive

--- Send `bytes` as one datagram to `to`.
public send_to: (provider: type is network, host: provider, socket: io.file,
                 bytes: []u8, to: address)
                -> none ! unreachable | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    try provider.send_to(host, socket, bytes, to)
end send_to

--- Receive the next datagram into `into` and say who sent it.
public receive_from: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      socket: io.file, into: []mut u8)
                     -> (received: datagram) ! timed_out | io.io_failed =
    received = try provider.receive_from(host, socket, into)
end receive_from

--- Stop receiving, sending or both on a connection: `io.sending` lets
--- the peer read the end of what was sent.
public shutdown: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                  connection: io.file, stopping: direction)
                 -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.shutdown(host, connection, stopping)
end shutdown

--- The address and port a socket is bound to: a listener's or a datagram
--- socket's port when it was bound to port 0.
public local_address: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                       socket: io.file) -> (at: address) ! io.io_failed =
    at = try provider.local_address(host, socket)
end local_address

--- The address and port of a connection's peer.
public peer_address: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      connection: io.file) -> (at: address) ! io.io_failed =
    at = try provider.peer_address(host, connection)
end peer_address

--- Limit how long a receive or an accept, and a send, waits on `socket`;
--- zero is no limit.  A limit that passes is `timed_out`.
public set_timeouts: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      socket: io.file, receive_limit: time.duration,
                      send_limit: time.duration) -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.set_timeouts(host, socket, receive_limit, send_limit)
end set_timeouts

--- Send a connection's small writes at once rather than gather them.
public set_no_delay: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      connection: io.file, on: bool) -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.set_no_delay(host, connection, on)
end set_no_delay

--- Close a socket.  Treat it as consumed even if closing reports
--- `io_failed`.
public close_socket: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      sink socket: io.file) -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.close_socket(host, socket)
end close_socket
